    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d981">

                <pb facs="1475" n="1461" sameAs="frus:frus1944v01/pg1461"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.0011 LCC/21: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in the Soviet Union</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Harriman</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary
                        of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Moscow</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1944-03-21T00:00:00+03:00">March 21,
                            1944—midnight</date>.<lb/>[Received March 22—11 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>961. Department’s 312, February 16, 4 p.m.<anchor n="14" xml:id="d981-f1"/> In
                    conversation with Vyshinski<anchor n="15" xml:id="d981-f2"/> on March 21st I
                    referred to the participation of the Soviet Government on the London Propaganda
                    Committee concerning which I had written to Molotov<anchor n="16"
                        xml:id="d981-f3"/> on February 26 and inquired whether the Foreign Office
                    had made any decision in the premises. Vyshinski stated that the subject was
                    complicated in substance and in its technical aspects. However, he indicated
                    that a reply to my note would be sent to the Embassy in the near future.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Harriman</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d981-f1" n="14" xml:id="d981-f1-content">
                <p>See footnote 13, above.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d981-f2" n="15" xml:id="d981-f2-content">
                <p>Andrey Yanuaryevich Vyshinsky, First Deputy People’s Commissar for Foreign
                    Affairs of the Soviet Union.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d981-f3" n="16" xml:id="d981-f3-content">
                <p>Vyacheslav Mikhailovich Molotov, People’s Commissar for Foreign Affairs of the
                    Soviet Union.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
